> Here is the full proposal, for your reference:
> ===
> ACCELERATED DUAL-DEGREE PROGRAM:  BACHELOR OF SCIENCE IN ECONOMICS /  MS ENVIRONMENTAL ECONOMICS (BS-ECON-MEE) 
> Last Updated: October 20, 2025 
> PART I: PROGRAM HISTORY 
> A. Statement of purpose 
> The School of Marine Science and Policy, in collaboration with the Department of Economics, proposes a new accelerated dual-degree program that combines the Bachelor of Science in Economics (BS-ECON) with the MS in Environmental Economics (MEE). This 4+1 program aims to equip students with essential skills in economic theory and policy analysis focused on environmental challenges. The degree focuses on the intersection of economics and the environment with the intention of training students to inform decision-making in environmental policy in a practical and socially beneficial way.
> Student Demand 
> Nationally, the demand for graduates with Master’s degrees in Environmental Economics has risen significantly over the past decade, corresponding with increased awareness of sustainability and climate-related issues. The graph below shows annual job postings in the Mid-Atlantic region that include skills provided by the Environmental Economics degree (Source: Market Research Memo, Jan. 2022).
> 
> Environmental Economics equips students with essential analytical skills, enabling them to apply theoretical frameworks to address real-world challenges related to environmental sustainability and resource management. As the job market increasingly favors individuals capable of using data to inform economic and ecological decisions, interest in economics and related fields has surged. Economics majors are notably inclined to critically assess environmental policies, contributing to roles focused on compliance, sustainability, and environmental planning.
> 
> The BS-ECON-MEE is closely aligned with the University of Delaware’s Strategic Pillar to expand interdisciplinary and global opportunities.
> 
> The School of Marine Science & Policy (SMSP) first began to offer the MS in Environmental Economics (MEE) in 2022, building on its historic strength of the Master in Marine Policy program. This 4+1 program will further grow and strengthen that program.
> 
> Currently, UD offers 4+1 programs for undergraduates in Economics to pursue an MS in Economics and to pursue a Master in Public Policy (MPP) degree in the Biden School. A further offering will particularly cater to students interested in continuing their economics studies while integrating it with environmental issues.
> 
> Given the increasing interest in sustainability and environmental issues among UD students, there is considerable latent demand for this program. This initiative not only addresses the evolving job market but also positions UD as a leader in interdisciplinary environmental education.
> B. Date of permanent status 
> We expect to accept students into the accelerated program beginning in Academic Year 2026-27,  with the first graduates expected in Academic Year 2027-28. 
> C. Degrees offered 
> The program jointly confers the Bachelor of Science in Economics (BS-ECON) or Honors  Bachelor of Science in Economics (HBS-ECON) and the MS in Environmental Economics (MEE).  
> PART II. ADMISSION 
> A. Admission requirements 
> Current undergraduate students enrolled in the BS in Economics or Honors BS in Economics are  eligible to apply to the accelerated BS-ECON-MEE program after completing 60 credits, which  is typically the Fall of their junior year. 
> Admission decisions for the BS-ECON-MEE program are made by the SMSP graduate committee. Students will be admitted based on enrollment availability, their perceived fit within the program, and their ability to meet the minimum recommended entrance requirements of an undergraduate GPA of 3.3 or higher (on a 4.0 scale) 
> B. Prior Degree Requirements 
> N/A 
> C. Application deadlines. 
> Applicants must submit their completed application through the Graduate Admissions website  (https://grad.udel.edu/apply/) by November 1 for the following spring semester start; or April 1  for the following fall semester start. Applications should designate desired admission into the  4+1 BS-ECON-MEE program. 
> D. Special competencies needed 
> 		None.
> 
> 
> E. Admission categories 
> All accepted students would have dual enrollment status in both the Bachelor of Science program in Economics and the MEE program. No provisional admissions will be granted. 
> F. Other documents required 
> The BS-ECON-MEE student application materials include: 
> • A completed University of Delaware graduate application. Students should select spring of the upcoming year  (junior year) as the application term.
> • 4+1 form required by the Graduate Office (http://www1.udel.edu/gradoffice/forms new/4%2B1.pdf). This form must be completed at the time of the application and  requires a plan of study that indicates the courses intended to be double-counted between  the two programs, as well as signatures from the student’s Economics advisor and the SMSP graduate curriculum committee chair. Students should select spring of the upcoming year (junior year)  as the application term. 
> • Three letters of recommendation. At least one letter should be from a faculty member in  student’s undergraduate program. Ideally, one letter would also be from a current or  former employer who can speak to the applicant’s professional capabilities. 
> • A current resume. 
> • A comprehensive UD unofficial undergraduate transcript. Students should have a GPA  of 3.3 and above. 
> • A statement of personal interests and objectives that clearly identifies the applicant’s  educational objectives and explains how admission to the program will facilitate the  applicant’s professional interest. 
> G. University statement 
> Admission to the graduate program is competitive. Those who meet stated requirements are not  guaranteed admission, nor are those who fail to meet all of those requirements necessarily  precluded from admission if they offer other appropriate strengths.
> 
> 
> PART III. ACADEMIC 
> A. Degree requirements
> Students in the 4+1 BS-ECON-MEE program must complete the degree requirements of the BS in  Economics and the degree requirements of the MEE with the exception of one course which has an approved replacement: ECON 422 Econometric Methods for Causal Analysis will be accepted in place of the SPPA 704 Advanced Quantitative Methods requirement, with an additional elective taking the place of the graduate credits.
> We propose that 915 credits be “double-counted” across the two degrees, as is the case with the 4+1 BS MPP program, which would eliminate one semester of full-time undergraduate study. This allows 4+1 students to have the same cohort experience as normal Masters students, sharing the same sequence of classes.
> The MEE curriculum and BS-ECON are repeated here for reference. 
> 1.Curriculum - MEE 
> The M.S. in Environmental Economics (MEE) degree requires 33 credits: a core of 21 credits, electives of  6 credits, and a 6-credit capstone sequencecourse. 
> Core Courses (21cr) 
> Complete 3 credits of economic theory:  
> Choose one of the following:
> ECON 811 Microeconomic Theory I
> APEC 801 Microeconomic Theory and Behavior
> Complete 9 credits of environment & resource economics: 
> MAST 675/ECON 675 Natural Resource Economics
> MAST 676/ECON 676 Environmental Economics
> MAST 688 Climate Change Economics
> Complete 3 credits of quantitative methods: 
> SPPA 704 Advanced Quantitative Methods
> BS-Economics-MEE students may substitute an elective if they have taken ECON 422 Econometric Methods for Causal Analysis
> Complete 3 credits of natural systems: 
> MAST 610 Coupling Human to Natural Systems
> Substitution of another science course is allowed with approval of the advisor and the SMSP graduate committee.
> 
> Complete 3 credits of policy analysis: 
> Choose one of the following:
> UAPP 701 Public Policy 
> UAPP 707 Public Policy Analysis 
> Substitution of another policy analysis course is allowed with approval of the advisor and the SMSP graduate committee.
> Electives (6cr) 
