> B. Advisement and Evaluation of Students 
> During the B.S. in Economics undergraduate portion of the program, students will have a Lerner professional advisor for the first two years and then a faculty advisor in the Department of Economics for the last two years. After the student is admitted to the 4+1  program, students will be appointed an MEE advisor who is responsible for ensuring that students  follow an appropriate plan of study. The student's advisor is responsible for monitoring student  progress and performance on a regular basis until the completion of all degree requirements. The  student prepares a plan of study that is approved by the advisor and that plan becomes the basis for  monitoring progress throughout the program. 
> Once a student is admitted to the 4+1 program, at the end of each semester the grades of all  students are reviewed by the director and the faculty. All students in the 4+1 program are expected  to maintain a 3.0 cumulative index on a 4.0 scale to remain in good standing in the program. In  cases of substandard performance, actions are initiated in accord with general University guidelines  regarding warning, probation, and termination.
> C. Timetable 
> 1. Academic Load 
> Students in the BS-ECON-MEE program will typically be enrolled full-time throughout both  degree programs. Full-time study for BS students is at least 12 credits per semester, typically  15-16 credits. Full-time study for an MEE student working 20 hours per week is 9 credits per  semester; 12-15 credits if working fewer hours. 
> Students would apply to the BS-ECON-MEE program after the completion of 60 credits, which is  typically in the fall of the 3rd year. MEE courses would be introduced incrementally to the  student’s program for the remainder of their 3rd and 4th years. Full-time MEE study should  commence in the 5th year. Sequencing and timing of courses should be discussed closely and  frequently with the student’s advisor. 
> Progress will be tracked each semester by the MEE graduate director, in concert with assessment  of other MEE students. 
