**assistant**:
> I agree: don’t add much. The flier is already doing the motivational work. What it needs now is a **clear action path** and maybe one compact eligibility/program-structure line so students know whether it applies to them.
> 
> I would revise it to something like this:
> 
> ---
> 
> ## Earn a BS in Economics + MS in Environmental Economics in 5 Years  
> ### New 4+1 Pathway for Economics Majors
> 
> The 4+1 pathway allows qualified Economics BS or Honors BS students to begin graduate coursework as undergraduates and complete a Master’s in Environmental Economics with one additional year of study.
> 
> - Build expertise in climate, energy, natural resources, sustainability, and policy  
> - Gain quantitative and policy skills for addressing environmental challenges  
> - Prepare for careers in government, consulting, nonprofits, research, industry, or further graduate study  
> - Eligible students may double-count selected courses toward both degrees  
> 
> ### What to do
> 
> **1. Plan early.**  
> Talk with your Economics advisor during sophomore year or early junior year to make sure your course plan fits the 4+1 pathway.
> 
> **2. Check eligibility.**  
> Students may apply after completing 60 credits. A **3.3 GPA or higher** is recommended.
> 
> **3. Apply through Graduate Admissions.**  
> Submit the UD graduate application and designate interest in the **4+1 BS-ECON-MEE program**.
> 
> **Application deadlines:**  
> **November 1** for spring start  
> **April 1** for fall start
> 
> **Questions?**  
> Contact: **[Name / email]**  
> Learn more: **[URL or QR code]**
> 
> ---
> 
> I would probably **replace** your current “Be prepared for careers…” bullet with the slightly more concrete career bullet above, and add the double-counting bullet. The “double-counted courses” point is important because it explains the value of the 4+1 without getting into the full curriculum.
> 
> I would **not** include the full list of required documents on the flier unless this is intended as an application handout rather than a recruitment flier. If you want one more compact line, you could add:
> 
> > Application materials include a plan of study, transcript, resume, statement of interests, and letters of recommendation.
> 
> But for a clean recruitment flier, I’d leave that to the QR code/webpage.
